**Do not compute these by hand.** `/roi` derives all four from the nine inputs
above and prints the arithmetic with the numbers substituted, because the first
question will be where the number came from and a typed figure cannot answer
it. A missing input produces no output rather than a zero that reads as a
result.

<!-- table:roi-model.buckets role=labels key=Bucket  answer=Effect -->

## The three buckets

**There are exactly three buckets a business cares about, and every deployed
system is measured against all three.** A model that reports only cost savings
is not a conservative model — it is an incomplete one, and the bucket it
usually omits is the one the sponsor is actually judged on.

| Bucket | Effect | Arithmetic or evidence | Measured / modelled / assumed | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Cost savings | | | | |
| Revenue uplift | | | | |
| Risk mitigation | | | | |

Leave a row empty only if you can say why it does not apply — and write that in
`Effect`. "Not applicable" with a reason is an answer; a blank cell is not.

<!-- table:roi-model.counter-metrics role=register id=Counter-metric -->

## Counter-metrics

What must **not** get worse. A pilot that improves throughput and quietly degrades quality has not succeeded, and without a counter-metric nobody notices until a customer does.

| Counter-metric | Baseline | Acceptable floor | Actual | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|

**Never drop these from the readout because they are unflattering.** Their absence is what a sceptical CFO looks for.

